IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mistake-domain`

§ `Reading four verdicts side by side`

-- NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS A RULE OF ANY JURISDICTION. Every rule lives in the
-- jurisdiction module that states it. This file holds the machinery for
-- comparing their answers, and one consequence -- what a verdict means for a
-- third party -- that no single jurisdiction module could state, because it
-- is a property of the VERDICT rather than of the test that produced it.
--
-- There is deliberately no record type collecting all four verdicts at once.
-- England refuses on a whole class of facts, and a record built from four
-- verdicts would propagate that refusal and hide the three answers that do
-- exist. The comparison is therefore made one jurisdiction at a time, which
-- is also how the case battery asserts it.

§§ `Naming a verdict`

-- A short name, so that two verdicts can be compared and so that the
-- comparison table prints something a reader recognises.

GIVEN v IS A `What becomes of the contract`
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE `the name of` v IS
    CONSIDER v
    WHEN `the contract stands`                                     THEN "stands"
    WHEN `the contract is void`                                    THEN "void"
    WHEN `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election` THEN "voidable"

GIVEN a IS A `What becomes of the contract`, b IS A `What becomes of the contract`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE a `reaches the same result as` b IF
    `the name of` a EQUALS `the name of` b

§§ `Whether the mistaken party gets anything`

-- The coarsest comparison, and the one a client asks first: am I stuck with
-- this contract? Void and voidable differ enormously in mechanism and hardly
-- at all in answer to that question.

GIVEN v IS A `What becomes of the contract`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the mistaken party is relieved` IF
    CONSIDER v
    WHEN `the contract stands` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `Why void and voidable are not the same relief`

-- Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA 2 at [76], on why
-- the Court of Appeal would not collapse the two limbs into a single
-- actual-knowledge rule:
--
--   "In view of the difficulties, one may be tempted to take a clear
--   simplistic approach, namely, where there is actual knowledge, the
--   contract would be void at common law. But where there is no actual
--   knowledge, the contract ought to be performed. There would then be no
--   room for equity to operate. But we believe that simplicity may not always
--   lead to a just result, especially where innocent third parties are
--   involved."
--
-- This is where that lands. Suppose the non-mistaken party resells to someone
-- who buys in good faith, for value, without notice of the mistake, before
-- the mistaken party has rescinded. A void contract passed him nothing to
-- sell, so the buyer gets nothing. A voidable one passed him title good until
-- avoided, so the buyer keeps it and the mistaken party's remedy is gone.
--
-- The consequence is stated here as the general rule and nothing finer. When
-- rescission is barred, what restitution follows, and how registration
-- statutes cut across it are all outside this corpus.

@export Decide whether someone who bought in good faith from the non-mistaken party, for value and without notice, before any rescission, keeps what they bought — given what the governing law does with the underlying contract.
GIVEN v IS A `What becomes of the contract`  @desc The verdict on the underlying contract, from one of the jurisdiction modules.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a good faith purchaser for value without notice keeps what he bought` IF
    CONSIDER v
    WHEN `the contract is void`                                    THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the contract stands`                                     THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election` THEN TRUE

§§ `Where two jurisdictions part company`

GIVEN a IS A `What becomes of the contract`, b IS A `What becomes of the contract`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE a `diverges from` b IF
    NOT (a `reaches the same result as` b)

-- The divergence worth naming separately: both jurisdictions relieve the
-- mistaken party, but by different mechanisms, so they differ only for the
-- third party. Singapore against Australia on an actually-known fundamental
-- mistake is the live instance.
GIVEN a IS A `What becomes of the contract`, b IS A `What becomes of the contract`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE a `differs from, only as to third parties,` b IF
        `the mistaken party is relieved` a
    AND `the mistaken party is relieved` b
    AND NOT (a `reaches the same result as` b)
